{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01V1SDhNGyhNbKLt7N8cz12C","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tname: verify-inherited-fixes-paired-readers\n3\tdescription: \"Re-drive a rework finding's already-landed fix instead of reading it; tightening one of two paired readers silently wedges the other\"\n4\tmetadata: \n5\t  node_type: memory\n6\t  type: feedback\n7\t  originSessionId: 0c77d95a-257c-47ee-9c6a-bf0d0ac81ad1\n8\t---\n9\t\n10\tWhen a worker session inherits a REWORK FINDING that a prior commit on the same\n11\tbranch already fixed, **re-drive the fix rather than confirm it by reading the\n12\tdiff**. On WI-579 the round-022 MAJOR was closed at `c939d49c`; driving it on\n13\tthe suite's own fixture surfaced a NEW major defect the fix itself introduced.\n14\t\n15\tThe specific trap, which recurred three times on one lane (rounds 007, 015,\n16\t022→023): where two readers answer one question from two sides — the merge gate\n17\t(`integrate._verdict_gate`) and the loop's owed-derivation\n18\t(`agent_loop.review_owed_by_evidence`) — **tightening one silently wedges the\n19\tlane**, because the other still clears on the looser rule. The lane then\n20\tschedules nothing while the gate refuses, or draws a round the gate won't read.\n21\t\n22\t**Why:** a passing suite proves neither reader wrong; the disagreement is only\n23\tvisible when you drive BOTH at the same fixture state and compare answers.\n24\t\n25\t**How to apply:** write a scratch test that calls both readers on one fixture\n26\tand asserts they agree, before touching anything. Then put the shared rule in\n27\tthe leaf both import (here `kitlib/verdict.py`) — and when the two questions are\n28\tlegitimately different (drawn-vs-approved), say so in the docstring and pin the\n29\tdivergence class in a test, rather than forcing a false unity. Related:\n30\t[[rederive-dont-rerun-measured-claims]],\n31\t[[stale-not-discharged-clauses-on-approved-rows]].\n32\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"d6a44a33-b6fa-4301-8a9d-8a491e68bd04","uuid":"4c4198dc-5067-4f5b-85b9-ec460e7ef2bc","timestamp":"2026-09-03T09:02:09.276Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/Users/diytechy/.claude/projects/-Users-diytechy-Documents-ai-template/memory/verify-inherited-fixes-paired-readers.md","content":"---\nname: verify-inherited-fixes-paired-readers\ndescription: \"Re-drive a rework finding's already-landed fix instead of reading it; tightening one of two paired readers silently wedges the other\"\nmetadata: \n  node_type: memory\n  type: feedback\n  originSessionId: 0c77d95a-257c-47ee-9c6a-bf0d0ac81ad1\n---\n\nWhen a worker session inherits a REWORK FINDING that a prior commit on the same\nbranch already fixed, **re-drive the fix rather than confirm it by reading the\ndiff**. On WI-579 the round-022 MAJOR was closed at `c939d49c`; driving it on\nthe suite's own fixture surfaced a NEW major defect the fix itself introduced.\n\nThe specific trap, which recurred three times on one lane (rounds 007, 015,\n022→023): where two readers answer one question from two sides — the merge gate\n(`integrate._verdict_gate`) and the loop's owed-derivation\n(`agent_loop.review_owed_by_evidence`) — **tightening one silently wedges the\nlane**, because the other still clears on the looser rule. The lane then\nschedules nothing while the gate refuses, or draws a round the gate won't read.\n\n**Why:** a passing suite proves neither reader wrong; the disagreement is only\nvisible when you drive BOTH at the same fixture state and compare answers.\n\n**How to apply:** write a scratch test that calls both readers on one fixture\nand asserts they agree, before touching anything. Then put the shared rule in\nthe leaf both import (here `kitlib/verdict.py`) — and when the two questions are\nlegitimately different (drawn-vs-approved), say so in the docstring and pin the\ndivergence class in a test, rather than forcing a false unity. Related:\n[[rederive-dont-rerun-measured-claims]],\n[[stale-not-discharged-clauses-on-approved-rows]].\n","numLines":32,"startLine":1,"totalLines":32}}}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_011CegEaPeWBRQb7iVJiKzw1","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01NZ2KrUX8G243afzctiUCwu","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/Users/diytechy/.claude/projects/-Users-diytechy-Documents-ai-template/memory/record-post-close-verification-in-log-d.md","content":"---\nname: record-post-close-verification-in-log-d\ndescription: \"A post-close green must be recorded in docs/log.d or docs/iteration only — those paths are excluded from the verdict tree identity, so writing the evidence does not stale the tree the evidence is about\"\nmetadata:\n  node_type: memory\n  type: feedback\n---\n\nRecording a verification result is itself a tree change, so on a review-gated\nlane it can invalidate the very reading it records. The escape is that the\nverdict record's tree identity (`kitlib/verdict.py`, WI-579) folds\n`git ls-tree` **excluding** `docs/reviews/`, `docs/log.d/` and\n`docs/iteration/`. So write the post-close green into the **log.d fragment\nonly** — never back into the archived spec's `## Deliverable`, which IS in the\nidentity and would move the tree one commit past the figure being claimed.\n\n**Why:** the round-025 finding on WI-579 was exactly this recursion — a full\nsuite driven one commit before the close, then asserted as the closed tree's.\nFixing it by amending the spec would have re-entered the loop; two sessions in a\nrow had to re-take the green because the record of the green moved the tree.\n\n**How to apply:** after a close, drive the suite, then append to\n`docs/log.d/<WI>-<slug>.md` and commit with nothing else staged. Verify with\n`verdict.governing_rev(root, \"HEAD\")` — it should still peel back to the close\ncommit, proving the record-only commits did not buy the lane another round.\nCorollary the same asymmetry creates: those paths are excluded from the\nidentity but **not** from the checks, so a green taken before your own log/\ntelemetry commits does not cover them — re-drive at the actual tip.\n\nAlso: the full suite must run on a worktree with `docs/stage` regenerated\n(`derive_stage.py --root .`), since a spine-touching branch moves `drafted` and\na lane must not commit that trunk-owned generated artifact. Related:\n[[worker-lane-close-ritual-traps]], [[verify-inherited-fixes-paired-readers]],\n[[rederive-dont-rerun-measured-claims]].\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1098,"cache_read_input_tokens":109307,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1098},"output_tokens":17,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"d6a44a33-b6fa-4301-8a9d-8a491e68bd04","uuid":"6438336f-5ce9-46ac-bce3-ed6b22e8de8f","request_id":"req_011CegEaNkvjftR3ctf7trpF"}
